--- c/.docs/domain/sms-import.md
+++ i/.docs/domain/sms-import.md
@@ -14,7 +14,7 @@ Banking SMS are read from the Android inbox (on demand, and in the background wh
 1. **SMS-001** An SMS becomes at most one pending entry, ever: messages are recognized by a key (provider id plus receive time) whatever the pending entry's status (pending, confirmed, dismissed); a scan never recreates a dismissed one, but the user can undo a dismissal, which sets that entry back to pending (re-resolving its category by the current rules, SMS-003). A scan reads only messages received after its start point; the stored scan point only moves forward.
 2. **SMS-002** A message is parsed by the first enabled template (by id) whose sender filter and keywords match (template regexes and keywords are case-insensitive); the type is the keyword that occurs first; the amount is parsed into `Money` with the app currency's minor units and must be above zero; without a date match the receive time is used. A template error (bad regex, unparseable date) skips the message and never aborts the scan.
 3. **SMS-003** A category rule matches the merchant case-insensitively and, when no rule matches it (or there is no merchant), the whole SMS text, as its match mode says: `contains` (the default, and what rules stored without a mode mean) finds the keyword anywhere, `word` only as a whole word (bounded by the text's ends or a character that is neither a letter nor a digit), `prefix` only at the start of the text. Only categories of the pending entry's type count; within each pass the longest keyword wins and the earlier rule wins a tie. A pending entry's category only ever comes from the rules (the user sets one only by confirming), so rules are re-applied to all pending entries whenever one is saved, edited or removed, in the same transaction as the rule write: each category becomes whatever the current rules resolve, possibly none. Rules are editable (keyword, category, mode). Keywords are unique case-insensitively: saving a new rule with an existing keyword updates that rule, an edit that would give a rule another rule's keyword is refused and writes nothing.
-4. **SMS-004** Confirming a pending entry creates the entry (ENTRY-001, ENTRY-005), marks the pending entry confirmed and, when the user gave a rule keyword (not blank after trimming; the form offers the whole merchant), saves the rule "keyword means the entry's category" (SMS-003), in one transaction. Confirm-all confirms only pending entries whose category has the pending entry's type.
+4. **SMS-004** Confirming a pending entry creates the entry (ENTRY-001, ENTRY-005), marks the pending entry confirmed and, when the user gave a rule keyword (not blank after trimming; the form offers the whole merchant), saves the rule "keyword means the entry's category" (SMS-003), in one transaction. Confirm-all confirms only pending entries whose category has the pending entry's type. A sealed category refuses the entry (ENTRY-008).
 5. **SMS-005** Deleting a category deletes its rules and clears it from pending entries in the same transaction.
 6. **SMS-006** A background scan (app start or resume) runs only when the read permission is already granted and a scan point exists; it never prompts and never asks for a start point. Scans run at most once a minute and never concurrently.
 7. **SMS-007** A template may read the currency code; a pending entry whose code differs from the app currency is never confirmed by confirm-all and is marked on the list.
